        "### **Experiment 1: Understanding Earthquake Ground-Motion Directionality**\n",
        "\n",
        "## Objective\n",
        "To understand how two perpendicular horizontal acceleration components combine and how their projected peak acceleration changes with orientation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Methodology\n",
        "Two synthetic horizontal acceleration time histories were generated over 10 seconds with a sampling interval of 0.01 seconds, producing 1,000 samples at a sampling frequency of 100 Hz. The components were defined using sinusoidal signals with different amplitudes and a phase difference.\n",
        "\n",
        "For orientations between 0° and 179°, the projected acceleration was computed as\n",
        "\n",
        "a_theta(t) = (t)cos(theta) + a_y(t)sin(theta)\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "The peak absolute projected acceleration was calculated for each orientation. A sensitivity experiment was then performed by doubling the amplitude of the X-component and repeating the orientation sweep.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Results\n",
        "The original synthetic signals produced a maximum sampled peak projected acceleration of 1.2446 m/s² at 17°. After doubling the X-component amplitude, the maximum increased to 2.4187 m/s² at 7°.\n",
        "\n",
        "The peak projected acceleration therefore increased by approximately 94.3%, and the maximizing sampled orientation shifted by 10°.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Observations\n",
        "The results demonstrate that the orientation associated with maximum projected acceleration depends on the relative characteristics of the two horizontal components. Changing one component's amplitude can affect both the peak response and the maximizing orientation.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Limitations\n",
        "This experiment used synthetic sinusoidal signals rather than recorded earthquake ground motions. The response measure was the peak absolute projected acceleration; it was not the oscillator-based RotD100 measure used in ground-motion directionality research. The experiment is a foundational demonstration and does not reproduce the original research paper's results.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Conclusion\n",
        "The experiment established a computational foundation for rotating horizontal acceleration components and measuring orientation-dependent peak acceleration. These concepts will be extended to real earthquake records and the directionality measures required for the research project."
